---
/**
 * Layer 2 landing page.
 *
 * This page previously held the whole guide. That content now lives at
 * /birth-time/, free and unGated, because it is the page that answers the
 * search and it was doing that job while also being asked to sell something —
 * two jobs, neither done cleanly.
 *
 * So the split is: /birth-time/ answers the question, this page offers the
 * process. It describes what the toolkit does, and it is honest that the
 * information itself is free elsewhere. The exchange being offered is a
 * working format and the monthly letter, not access to withheld facts.
 *
 * There is a plain download link as well as the signup. Someone who does not
 * want the newsletter should not have to pretend they do, and a lead magnet
 * that traps people is a poor first impression from a practice whose whole
 * position is candour.
 */
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import NewsletterForm from '../components/NewsletterForm.astro';
import BirthTimeConfidence from '../components/BirthTimeConfidence.astro';
import { scenes } from '../config/images';
import { site } from '../config/site';

const crumbs = [{ label: 'Birth Time Toolkit', href: '/birth-time-toolkit/' }];
const pdfPath = '/downloads/birth-time-toolkit.pdf';
const description =
  'A worksheet set for finding your birth time: record search checklist, family interview prompts, a way to compare times that disagree, and a summary to bring to a reading.';

const schema = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'WebPage',
    name: 'The Birth Time Toolkit',
    description,
    url: new URL('/birth-time-toolkit/', site.url).href,
    inLanguage: 'en-US',
    isPartOf: { '@type': 'WebSite', name: site.name, url: site.url },
    publisher: { '@type': 'Organization', name: site.name, url: site.url },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Birth Time Toolkit"
  description={description}
  path="/birth-time-toolkit/"
  breadcrumbs={crumbs}
  schema={schema}
>
  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={crumbs} />

  <section class="hero hero--split">
    <div class="container">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Free worksheet set</p>
        <h1>The Birth Time Toolkit</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          Knowing where to look is one thing. Actually working through it — the offices, the
          relatives, the two times that don't agree — is another. This is the part that keeps you
          organised while you do it.
        </p>
      </div>
      <img
        class="hero__img"
        src={scenes.newsletterLetters.src}
        srcset={scenes.newsletterLetters.srcset}
        sizes="100vw"
        alt={scenes.newsletterLetters.alt}
        width={scenes.newsletterLetters.width}
        height={scenes.newsletterLetters.height}
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 46rem;">
      <div class="prose flow">
        <h2>What's in it</h2>
        <p>
          Eight sections, printable, designed to be filled in as you go rather than read once and
          filed.
        </p>
      </div>
      <ul class="assurances" style="margin-top: var(--space-6);">
        <li><strong>What you already know</strong> — a place to write down the time you were told, who told you, and how sure they were.</li>
        <li><strong>Record search checklist</strong> — the offices and documents worth trying, in the order most likely to work, with room to log who you contacted and what they said.</li>
        <li><strong>Family interview sheets</strong> — prompts that get better answers than "what time was I born?", with space for two conversations.</li>
        <li><strong>When times disagree</strong> — a comparison table for candidate times, so you keep all of them instead of guessing early.</li>
        <li><strong>A confidence scale</strong> — five plain labels, from <em>documented</em> to <em>unknown</em>, that stop a guess quietly becoming a fact.</li>
        <li><strong>If it stays unknown</strong> — what a reading can and cannot do, without the drama.</li>
        <li><strong>Rectification, honestly</strong> — what the technique is, and what it cannot promise.</li>
        <li><strong>A reading preparation sheet</strong> — the summary to send me, including the dated life events a rectifier would ask for anyway.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/*
    The confidence check sits here — after the reader knows what the toolkit is,
    before they are asked for an email address.

    It goes on this page rather than on /birth-time/ or a new /tools/ URL, for
    two reasons. /birth-time/ is the page built to rank and it is already long;
    a widget there would push the record-retrieval prose further down the
    search result for no gain this page cannot deliver. And the birth-time topic
    was deliberately split into two layers on 2026-08-08 — a fourth URL would
    undo that.

    What it does for this page: /birth-time-toolkit/ was previously a
    description of a PDF and a form, with nothing for a reader to do. The check
    demonstrates the toolkit's own method in ninety seconds and hands back a
    labelled position with a next step, which is a better argument for the
    download than any paragraph about it.
  */}
  <section class="section section--sea">
    <div class="container" style="max-width: 46rem;">
      <div class="prose flow" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-8);">
        <p class="eyebrow">Ninety seconds</p>
        <h2>How good is the time you have?</h2>
        <p>
          Three quick questions about where your birth time came from. This doesn't work out what
          your time <em>is</em> — it works out how much weight it can carry, which is the thing
          that changes how I read a chart. You'll get one of the five labels from the toolkit's own
          confidence scale, and the section that goes with it.
        </p>
        <p class="card__meta">
          Nothing is stored and nothing is sent. I never ask for your birth date, your birth time,
          or where you were born.
        </p>
      </div>
      <BirthTimeConfidence />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container feature-split">
      <div class="flow">
        <p class="eyebrow">Two ways to get it</p>
        <h2 id="get-the-toolkit">Have it as a PDF</h2>
        <p>
          Join The Sky This Month and I'll send the toolkit through — useful at the records office,
          or to forward to whoever might remember. One letter a month after that: what's moving
          overhead, what it tends to mean, and one practical thing to do about it. Unsubscribe
          anytime.
        </p>
        <NewsletterForm source="birth-time-toolkit" labelledby="get-the-toolkit" />
        <p class="card__meta" style="margin-top: var(--space-4);">
          Rather not subscribe? <a href={pdfPath} data-toolkit-download download>Download it
          directly</a> — no address needed. You can also
          <a href="/birth-time-toolkit/worksheets/">work through it in your browser</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="flow">
        <div class="callout">
          <p style="margin: 0;">
            <strong>The information is free either way.</strong> Everything the toolkit is built on
            is on <a href="/birth-time/">how to find your birth time</a>, in full, with nothing held
            back. What you're getting here is the format — the checklists and worksheets that turn
            it into something you can work through.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container grid grid--3">
      <article class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-lg); margin: 0;">
          <a href="/birth-time/">How to find your birth time</a>
        </h2>
        <p style="margin: 0;">The full guide, free and unGated.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-lg); margin: 0;">
          <a href="/prepare-for-your-reading/">Prepare for your reading</a>
        </h2>
        <p style="margin: 0;">Everything else worth bringing.</p>
      </article>
      <article class="card">
        <h2 style="font-size: var(--text-lg); margin: 0;">
          <a href="/reading-finder/">Reading Finder</a>
        </h2>
        <p style="margin: 0;">Five questions, and I'll say which reading fits.</p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  document
    .querySelector('[data-toolkit-download]')
    ?.addEventListener('click', () =>
      (window as any).mlTrack?.('toolkit_download', { toolkit: 'birth-time' })
    );
</script>
